        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Memory Foam, Hybrid, Latex or Innerspring: Which Type Is Best?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mattresses fall into four broad families, and the type tells you more about how a bed will feel than its marketing name does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory foam</h3>

          <p>
            Memory foam softens with body heat and contours closely, which gives strong pressure relief and very little motion transfer between partners. The trade-offs are a slow, sinking feel that some people find hard to move in, and more retained heat. It suits side sleepers and couples with different schedules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid puts several inches of foam or latex over a layer of individually wrapped coils. You get the cushioning of foam with the bounce, edge support and airflow of springs. Hybrids are heavier and usually cost more than all-foam beds, but they are the most versatile type and the best fit for most sleepers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Latex</h3>

          <p>
            Latex is buoyant rather than sinking, sleeps cooler than memory foam and tends to last a long time. Natural latex beds are expensive and heavy. Most shoppers meet latex as a comfort layer in a hybrid or as a topper rather than as a whole mattress.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Innerspring</h3>

          <p>
            Traditional innersprings use a coil unit with a thin padded top. They are cool and bouncy but offer the least pressure relief, and cheap open-coil designs transfer a lot of motion. Today most good spring beds are sold as hybrids, with real comfort layers on top.
          </p>

