        <div id="firmness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Firmness Should You Choose for Your Sleep Position?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Makers describe firmness in words or on a 1 to 10 scale, where 1 is very soft and 10 is very firm. The scales are not standardized between brands, so treat them as a rough guide and lean on the trial period to settle the question.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers usually do best between soft-medium and medium. The shoulder and hip need to sink an inch or two so the spine stays straight; on a firm bed those two points carry all the weight and can ache by morning. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">side sleeper guide</a> covers hip and shoulder pain in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Back sleepers usually suit medium-firm. The goal is support under the lumbar curve without the hips sinking. Beds with zoned support, firmer under the hips and softer at the shoulders, are worth a look if you wake with lower back stiffness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stomach sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Stomach sleepers need the firmest surface of anyone, typically firm or medium-firm with a thin comfort layer. When the hips sink, the lower back arches, which is the most common cause of stomach sleepers waking up sore.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            If you move between positions, choose medium-firm with a responsive top layer, such as a hybrid or latex, so changing position does not feel like climbing out of a hole.
          </p>

