        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Does Body Weight Change Which Mattress You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weight changes both how firm a mattress feels and how long it lasts. A sleeper under about 130 pounds barely compresses the comfort layers, so a medium-firm bed can feel hard and offer little pressure relief. Lighter sleepers often prefer one step softer than the position chart suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sleepers between roughly 130 and 230 pounds are who most mattresses are designed around, and the stated firmness will feel close to what the maker intends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above about 230 pounds, the comfort foam compresses further and the support core does more of the work. That calls for a firmer feel, a taller mattress (12 inches or more), denser foams and a sturdier coil unit. It also makes edge support matter more, because sitting on the side of the bed puts a lot of load on a small area. Some makers build dedicated plus-size models with reinforced coils and thicker transition layers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Couples with different weights</h3>

          <p>
            When partners differ by 60 pounds or more, a hybrid with pocketed coils is usually the best compromise, because the individual coils respond to each body separately. Some brands also sell split-firmness models in king sizes, and a <a href="/best-mattress-topper/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">mattress topper</a> on one side can soften things for the lighter partner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Mattresses Are Best If You Sleep Hot?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat comes from two places: the foam directly under you, which insulates, and the lack of airflow through the mattress. Memory foam is the worst offender because it softens with heat and wraps around the body. Hybrids are cooler because air can move through the coil layer, and latex is cooler than memory foam because it is open-celled and does not hug as closely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Makers fight heat with gel infusions, phase-change covers, perforated foam and graphite or copper particles. These features help a little, and owners commonly report that a cooling cover feels cool to the touch at bedtime. None of them turns a dense memory foam bed into a cool one through a warm night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What actually helps</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a hybrid or latex bed over all-foam, avoid very soft memory foam that you sink deep into, and pay attention to the cover fabric. Breathable sheets, such as percale cotton or linen, make a bigger difference than most mattress cooling claims. If you already own a hot foam bed, a latex topper is a cheaper fix than a new mattress.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is a Mattress in a Box as Good as a Store Mattress?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the beds on this list arrive compressed and rolled in a box. Compression does not damage well-made foam or pocketed coils, and the in-a-box model is how most online brands keep prices down: no showroom, no commissioned salesperson and lower shipping costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The real difference is how you judge the bed. A store lets you lie down for five minutes, which tells you very little about how a mattress feels after a week. A box mattress gives you a home trial instead, usually 100 nights or more, which is a far better test as long as you understand the return rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Unboxing and off-gassing</h3>

          <p>
            Expect a new foam mattress to need 24 to 72 hours to fully expand, and expect a chemical smell for the first day or two. Certifications such as CertiPUR-US, which most of our picks list, cover foam content and emissions limits. Unbox it in a ventilated room, and do not judge the feel on the first night: foam softens noticeably over the first few weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and handling</h3>

          <p>
            Queen hybrids can weigh 90 to 130 pounds in the box. Plan on two people to carry one upstairs, and open the plastic on the bed frame rather than on the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Mattress Trials and Warranties Really Cover?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A home trial is the most valuable feature of any online mattress, and the fine print varies. Many brands require you to keep the bed for a minimum period, often 30 nights, before you can return it, because your body needs time to adjust to a new surface. Some charge a return or pickup fee, some donate the returned bed, and Amazon purchases may follow different return terms from buying direct, so read the listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranties are about defects, not comfort. The key clause is the sag or impression depth that counts as a defect, commonly somewhere between 0.75 and 1.5 inches. A bed with a shallow threshold is better protected. Most warranties also require a supportive foundation or frame and are voided by stains, so a mattress protector is cheap insurance.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Give a new mattress three to four weeks before you decide.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Your back and hips take time to adjust, and foam softens as it breaks in. Judge the bed after a month, not after the first night, and keep a note of any return deadline so it does not slip past.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Long marketing warranties, such as lifetime or forever coverage, usually become prorated or narrow after the first ten years. A clear ten-year non-prorated warranty is worth more than a vague lifetime promise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Should You Spend on a Mattress?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price tracks materials more than comfort. Budget all-foam beds under about $400 for a queen can be comfortable for a lighter sleeper or a guest room, but their thinner, lower-density foams tend to soften and sag sooner. Owners commonly report budget beds feeling noticeably softer within two or three years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle of the market, roughly $600 to $1,200 for a queen, is where most people get the best value: real pocketed coils, denser foams and long trials. Most of our picks sit here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premium beds above that add thicker comfort layers, zoned coils, better edge support and plusher covers. They make the most sense for heavier sleepers, people with chronic pain who want the extra support features, and anyone who plans to keep a bed for ten years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to buy</h3>

          <p>
            Mattress prices move constantly, and the big online brands discount most around major holidays. If a bed you like is on sale, the discount is real, but a list price that is always on sale is the actual price. Compare the price per year you expect to keep the bed rather than chasing the biggest percentage off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you spend, budget for a protector and check that your existing frame or foundation is supportive. Many warranties require slats no more than a few inches apart, and a sagging old box spring will make a new mattress feel worn out within months. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-seniors/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold no-underline hover:underline">guide for seniors</a> also covers bed height and edge support, which change which mattress is easiest to live with.
          </p>
        </div>

